1
00:00:06,200 --> 00:00:10,770
To see what one ramp actually computes, let's draw it as a little pipeline.

2
00:00:10,770 --> 00:00:17,533
The input is scaled by its weight, then a bias is added, and then we take the max with zero.

3
00:00:17,533 --> 00:00:18,960
Feed in one point five.

4
00:00:18,960 --> 00:00:22,434
Doubling it gives three, and subtracting one leaves two.

5
00:00:22,434 --> 00:00:27,933
That's positive, so it passes straight through, and the output is two.

6
00:00:27,933 --> 00:00:30,518
Now try a smaller input, zero point two.

7
00:00:30,518 --> 00:00:35,883
Doubling it gives zero point four, and subtracting one leaves minus zero point six.

8
00:00:35,883 --> 00:00:41,900
That's negative, so the max with zero wipes it out, and the output is zero.

9
00:00:41,900 --> 00:00:46,600
So one unit is three cheap operations, and only the last one bends anything.

10
00:00:46,600 --> 00:00:55,333
That matters in practice, because hardware built for multiplying and adding can run huge numbers of these at once.

11
00:00:55,333 --> 00:01:00,214
There's no reason to stop at one ramp, so let's put four of them side by side.

12
00:01:00,214 --> 00:01:06,408
Each ramp is now a hidden unit with its own weight and bias, and all of them read the same input x.

13
00:01:06,408 --> 00:01:11,477
Then a readout adds them up, each with its own output weight, plus one more bias.

14
00:01:11,477 --> 00:01:15,666
The notes call this equation one point seven.

15
00:01:15,666 --> 00:01:17,958
Let's push one point five through it.

16
00:01:17,958 --> 00:01:23,470
Each unit first computes its weight times x, plus its bias, and we'll call that number z.

17
00:01:23,470 --> 00:01:27,744
ReLU then lets three of them through and sets the second one to zero.

18
00:01:27,744 --> 00:01:35,633
Finally the readout weighs each output, adds them up with its bias, and out comes three point five.

19
00:01:35,633 --> 00:01:42,204
Going unit by unit gets tedious, so we stack the weights into a matrix and the biases into a vector,

20
00:01:42,204 --> 00:01:45,424
and then one multiply does all the units at once.

21
00:01:45,424 --> 00:01:47,855
This first matrix is tall and skinny.

22
00:01:47,855 --> 00:01:52,966
It has d rows and a single column, because our input is just one number.

23
00:01:52,966 --> 00:01:55,530
ReLU then acts on each entry separately.

24
00:01:55,530 --> 00:02:02,388
After that the second matrix, which is a single row, squashes the four activations back down to one number.

25
00:02:02,388 --> 00:02:11,066
So the whole layer costs just eight multiply-adds, and with vector inputs or outputs only the shapes change.

26
00:02:11,066 --> 00:02:15,884
Here's the whole layer from before, drawn as a function of its input x.

27
00:02:15,884 --> 00:02:20,701
You can see it bend at the knots, which sit at zero, one half, and one.

28
00:02:20,701 --> 00:02:22,940
But what if we deleted the ReLUs?

29
00:02:22,940 --> 00:02:29,833
Then two affine maps in a row just multiply out into a single one, with one matrix and one bias.

30
00:02:29,833 --> 00:02:37,000
With our numbers, the weights multiply out to four and the biases add up to minus two, so what's left is just a line.

31
00:02:37,000 --> 00:02:41,288
We used four units and two layers, and all we got was a straight line.

32
00:02:41,288 --> 00:02:44,866
Without the bend, depth buys us nothing.

33
00:02:44,866 --> 00:02:50,147
And that's why deep networks alternate, going affine, ReLU, affine, ReLU.

34
00:02:50,147 --> 00:02:56,833
The ReLUs in between are what stop the layers from merging into one.

